<text id="1768146" parent="1768142" visible="false" ontop="true" fixed="true" proportional="false" useemptyalttag="true" publishasimage="true" defaulttext="none">
<name>ann_text</name>
<lastupdated>1769442738</lastupdated>
<possize>
<point>
<x>212</x>
<y>212</y>
</point>
<size>
<cx>609</cx>
<cy>217</cy>
</size>
</possize>
<cssclasses>hideMe a_text</cssclasses>
<opacity>100</opacity>
<html>span.text1768146Font1 { font-size:16px; font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;, sans-serif; color:#010101;}&#x0A;&lt;p  legLH=&quot;1.127&quot; style=&quot;margin-left:0px;text-indent:0px;line-height:1.080;margin-top:0px;margin-bottom:0px;text-align:left;&quot; &gt;&#x0A;&lt;span class=&quot;text1768146Font1&quot;  &gt;XIAFLEX provides clinicians the opportunity to offer patients a Food and Drug Administration (FDA) nonsurgical prescription treatment option for appropriate patients with Dupuytren&apos;s contracture. XIAFLEX is indicated for the treatment of adult patients with Dupuytren&apos;s contracture with a palpable cord. As described in the Clinical Studies section, the efficacy of XIAFLEX was evaluated in 2 randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled trials in 374 adult patients with Dupuytren&apos;s contracture, otherwise known as Studies 1 and 2 in the PI. In Studies 1 and 2, a greater proportion of XIAFLEX-treated patients compared to placebo-treated patients achieved the primary endpoint, which was to evaluate the proportion of patients who achieved a reduction in contracture of the selected primary joint (metacarpophalangeal [MP] or proximal interphalangeal [PIP]) to within 0Â° to 5Â° of normal, 30 days after the last injection of that joint on Days 30, 60, or 90 (after up to 3 injections). &amp;nbsp;&#x0A;&lt;/span &gt;&lt;/p &gt;&#x0A;</html>
<rtf>{\rtf1\ansi\deflang1033\ftnbj\uc1\deff1&#x0A;{\fonttbl{\f0 \froman \fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1 \fswiss Arial;}}&#x0A;{\colortbl\red0\green0\blue0 ;\red255\green255\blue255 ;\red0\green0\blue0 ;}&#x0A;{\stylesheet{\f1\fs24\cf2\cb1\ulc2 Normal;}{\cs1\cf2\cb1\ulc2 Default Paragraph Font;}{\s2\snext0\cf2\cb1\ulc2 ;}}&#x0A;{\*\revtbl{Unknown;}}&#x0A;\paperw12240\paperh15840\margl1800\margr1800\margt1440\margb1440\headery720\footery720\deftab360\formshade\aendnotes\aftnnrlc\pgbrdrhead\pgbrdrfoot&#x0A;\sectd\pgwsxn12240\pghsxn15840\marglsxn1800\margrsxn1800\margtsxn1440\margbsxn1440\headery720\footery720\sbkpage\pgncont\pgndec&#x0A;\plain\plain\f1\fs24\ql\lang1033\hich\f1\loch\f1 XIAFLEX provides clinicians the opportunity to offer patients a Food and Drug Administration (FDA) nonsurgical prescription treatment option for appropriate patients with Dupuytren&apos;s contracture. XIAFLEX &#x0A;is indicated for the treatment of adult patients with Dupuytren&apos;s contracture with a palpable cord. As described in the Clinical Studies section, the efficacy of XIAFLEX was evaluated in 2 randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled trials in 374 adult &#x0A;patients with Dupuytren&apos;s contracture, otherwise known as Studies 1 and 2 in the PI. In Studies 1 and 2, a greater proportion of XIAFLEX-treated patients compared to placebo-treated patients achieved the primary endpoint, which was to evaluate the proportion &#x0A;of patients who achieved a reduction in contracture of the selected primary joint (metacarpophalangeal [MP] or proximal interphalangeal [PIP]) to within 0\u176 \&apos;b0 to 5\u176 \&apos;b0 of normal, 30 days after the last injection of that joint on Days 30, 60, &#x0A;or 90 (after up to 3 injections). \plain\f1\fs24  \par}&#x0A;</rtf>
<raw>﻿XIAFLEX provides clinicians the opportunity to offer patients a Food and Drug Administration (FDA) nonsurgical prescription treatment option for appropriate patients with Dupuytren&apos;s contracture. XIAFLEX is indicated for the treatment of adult patients with Dupuytren&apos;s contracture with a palpable cord. As described in the Clinical Studies section, the efficacy of XIAFLEX was evaluated in 2 randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled trials in 374 adult patients with Dupuytren&apos;s contracture, otherwise known as Studies 1 and 2 in the PI. In Studies 1 and 2, a greater proportion of XIAFLEX-treated patients compared to placebo-treated patients achieved the primary endpoint, which was to evaluate the proportion of patients who achieved a reduction in contracture of the selected primary joint (metacarpophalangeal [MP] or proximal interphalangeal [PIP]) to within 0° to 5° of normal, 30 days after the last injection of that joint on Days 30, 60, or 90 (after up to 3 injections).  </raw>
<numoflines>12</numoflines>
<numpubbullets>0</numpubbullets>
<textpublishlang>Inherit</textpublishlang>
<marginsize>0</marginsize>
</text>
