<html>span.text1857359Font1 { font-size:16px; font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;, sans-serif; color:#010101;}&#x0A;span.text1857359Font2 { font-size:16px; font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;, sans-serif; color:#010101; font-weight:bold;}&#x0A;&lt;p  legLH=&quot;1.127&quot; style=&quot;margin-left:0px;text-indent:0px;line-height:1.080;margin-top:0px;margin-bottom:0px;text-align:left;&quot; &gt;&#x0A;&lt;span class=&quot;text1857359Font1&quot;  &gt;XIAFLEX should only be injected into the collagen cord with a MP or PIP joint contracture, not the joint. XIAFLEX must be reconstituted with the provided diluent prior to use, and the dose depends on the location of the palpable cord, ie, a palpable cord with a contracture of an MP joint or a PIP joint. The volume of sterile diluent for reconstitution and the reconstituted XIAFLEX solution to be used in the intralesional injection for each location is shown in Table 1 of the PI on a subsequent page. Each vial of XIAFLEX and sterile diluent should only be used for a single injection. If 2 joints on the same hand are to be treated during the same treatment visit, separate vials and syringes should be used for each reconstitution and injection. &#x0A;&lt;/span &gt;&lt;/p &gt;&#x0A;&lt;p  legLH=&quot;1.127&quot; style=&quot;margin-left:0px;text-indent:0px;line-height:1.080;margin-top:0px;margin-bottom:0px;text-align:left;&quot; &gt;&#x0A;&lt;span class=&quot;text1857359Font1&quot; style=&quot;background-color:transparent&quot; &gt;&#x0A;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span &gt;&lt;/p &gt;&#x0A;&lt;p  style=&quot;margin-left:0px;text-indent:0px;margin-top:0px;margin-bottom:0px;text-align:left;&quot; &gt;&#x0A;&lt;span class=&quot;text1857359Font1&quot;  &gt;The figure below shows the &lt;/span &gt;&lt;span class=&quot;text1857359Font2&quot;  &gt;key bones and joints of the human hand&lt;/span &gt;&lt;span class=&quot;text1857359Font1&quot;  &gt;. &#x0A;&lt;/span &gt;&lt;/p &gt;&#x0A;</html>
<rtf>{\rtf1\ansi\deflang1033\ftnbj\uc1\deff1&#x0A;{\fonttbl{\f0 \froman \fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1 \fswiss Arial;}}&#x0A;{\colortbl\red0\green0\blue0 ;\red255\green255\blue255 ;\red0\green0\blue0 ;}&#x0A;{\stylesheet{\f1\fs24\cf2\cb1\ulc2 Normal;}{\cs1\cf2\cb1\ulc2 Default Paragraph Font;}{\s2\snext0\cf2\cb1\ulc2 ;}}&#x0A;{\*\revtbl{Unknown;}}&#x0A;\paperw12240\paperh15840\margl1800\margr1800\margt1440\margb1440\headery720\footery720\deftab360\formshade\aendnotes\aftnnrlc\pgbrdrhead\pgbrdrfoot&#x0A;\sectd\pgwsxn12240\pghsxn15840\marglsxn1800\margrsxn1800\margtsxn1440\margbsxn1440\headery720\footery720\sbkpage\pgncont\pgndec&#x0A;\plain\plain\f1\fs24\ql XIAFLEX should only be injected into the collagen cord with a MP or PIP joint contracture, not the joint. XIAFLEX must be reconstituted with the provided diluent prior to use, and the dose depends on the location of the palpable &#x0A;cord, ie, a palpable cord with a contracture of an MP joint or a PIP joint. The volume of sterile diluent for reconstitution and the reconstituted XIAFLEX solution to be used in the intralesional injection for each location is shown in Table 1 of the &#x0A;PI on a subsequent page. Each vial of XIAFLEX and sterile diluent should only be used for a single injection. If 2 joints on the same hand are to be treated during the same treatment visit, separate vials and syringes should be used for each reconstitution &#x0A;and injection. \lang1033\hich\f1\loch\f1\par\par The figure below shows the \b key bones and joints of the human hand\b0 . \plain\f1\fs24\par}&#x0A;</rtf>
<raw>﻿XIAFLEX should only be injected into the collagen cord with a MP or PIP joint contracture, not the joint. XIAFLEX must be reconstituted with the provided diluent prior to use, and the dose depends on the location of the palpable cord, ie, a palpable cord with a contracture of an MP joint or a PIP joint. The volume of sterile diluent for reconstitution and the reconstituted XIAFLEX solution to be used in the intralesional injection for each location is shown in Table 1 of the PI on a subsequent page. Each vial of XIAFLEX and sterile diluent should only be used for a single injection. If 2 joints on the same hand are to be treated during the same treatment visit, separate vials and syringes should be used for each reconstitution and injection. &#x0A;The figure below shows the key bones and joints of the human hand. </raw>
