<html>span.text1865930Font1 { font-size:21px; font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;, sans-serif; color:#000000; font-weight:bold;}&#x0A;span.text1865930Font2 { font-size:13px; font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;, sans-serif; color:#000000; font-style:italic;}&#x0A;span.text1865930Font3 { font-size:16px; font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;, sans-serif; color:#010101;}&#x0A;&lt;p  legLH=&quot;1.192&quot; style=&quot;margin-left:0px;text-indent:0px;line-height:1.192;margin-top:0px;margin-bottom:0px;text-align:left;&quot; &gt;&#x0A;&lt;span class=&quot;text1865930Font1&quot;  &gt;Dosing Note&lt;br /&gt;&lt;/span &gt;&lt;span class=&quot;text1865930Font2&quot;  &gt;Scroll for more information.&lt;/span &gt;&lt;span class=&quot;text1865930Font3&quot; style=&quot;background-color:transparent&quot; &gt;&#x0A;&lt;/span &gt;&lt;/p &gt;&#x0A;</html>
<rtf>{\rtf1\ansi\deflang1033\ftnbj\uc1\deff1&#x0A;{\fonttbl{\f0 \froman \fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1 \fswiss Arial;}}&#x0A;{\colortbl\red0\green0\blue0 ;\red255\green255\blue255 ;\red0\green0\blue0 ;}&#x0A;{\stylesheet{\f1\fs24\cf2\cb1\ulc2 Normal;}{\cs1\cf2\cb1\ulc2 Default Paragraph Font;}}&#x0A;{\*\revtbl{Unknown;}}&#x0A;\paperw12240\paperh15840\margl1800\margr1800\margt1440\margb1440\headery720\footery720\deftab360\formshade\aendnotes\aftnnrlc\pgbrdrhead\pgbrdrfoot&#x0A;\sectd\pgwsxn12240\pghsxn15840\marglsxn1800\margrsxn1800\margtsxn1440\margbsxn1440\headery720\footery720\sbkpage\pgncont\pgndec&#x0A;\plain\plain\f1\fs24\ql\cf2\fs32\b Dosing Note\lang1033\hich\f1\loch\f1\line\lang1033\hich\f1\loch\f1\fs20\b0\i Scroll for more information.\plain\f1\fs24\par}&#x0A;</rtf>
<raw>﻿Dosing Note&#x0F;Scroll for more information.</raw>
<numoflines>2</numoflines>
<numpubbullets>0</numpubbullets>
<textpublishlang>Inherit</textpublishlang>
<marginsize>0</marginsize>
</text>
<text id="1865931" parent="1865925" visible="false" ontop="true" fixed="true" proportional="false" defaulttext="none" verticalscroll="true">
<name>ann_text</name>
<lastupdated>1769548096</lastupdated>
<possize>
<point>
<x>212</x>
<y>213</y>
</point>
<size>
<cx>618</cx>
<cy>224</cy>
</size>
</possize>
<cssclasses>hideMe a_text</cssclasses>
<opacity>100</opacity>
<html>span.text1865931Font1 { font-size:16px; font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;, sans-serif; color:#010101; font-weight:normal; font-style:normal; text-decoration:normal;}&#x0A;li.text1865931Font1 { font-size:16px; font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;, sans-serif; color:#010101; font-weight:normal; font-style:normal; text-decoration:normal;}&#x0A;span.text1865931Font2 { font-size:16px; font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;, sans-serif; color:#010101; font-weight:bold; font-style:normal; text-decoration:normal;}&#x0A;li.text1865931Font2 { font-size:16px; font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;, sans-serif; color:#010101; font-weight:bold; font-style:normal; text-decoration:normal;}&#x0A;span.text1865931Font3 { font-size:16px; font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;, sans-serif; color:#0000ff; font-weight:bold; font-style:normal; text-decoration:underline;}&#x0A;li.text1865931Font3 { font-size:16px; font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;, sans-serif; color:#0000ff; font-weight:bold; font-style:normal; text-decoration:underline;}&#x0A;span.text1865931Font4 { font-size:16px; font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;, sans-serif; color:#000000; font-weight:bold; font-style:normal; text-decoration:normal;}&#x0A;li.text1865931Font4 { font-size:16px; font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;, sans-serif; color:#000000; font-weight:bold; font-style:normal; text-decoration:normal;}&#x0A;&lt;p  legLH=&quot;1.127&quot; style=&quot;margin-left:0px;text-indent:0px;line-height:1.080;margin-top:0px;margin-bottom:8px;text-align:left;&quot; &gt;&#x0A;&lt;span class=&quot;text1865931Font1&quot;  &gt;At the follow-up visit anywhere from 24 to 72 hours after the injection(s), if a contracture still remains, passive finger extension may be performed on each treated joint to facilitate cord disruption. Key points to note include the following: &#x0A;&lt;/span &gt;&lt;/p &gt;&#x0A;&lt;ul  style=&quot;list-style-position:outside;text-align:left;&quot; &gt;&#x0A;&lt;li  class=&quot;text1865931Font1&quot; data-bltIndnt=&quot;0&quot; style=&quot;line-height:1.127;position:relative;margin-left:15px;margin-top:0px;margin-bottom:8px; list-style-image:url(&apos;images/text1865931SquareBulletFont7.png&apos;); padding-left:7px;&quot;&gt;&lt;!--{ &#x0A;data-bltIndnt:0&#x0A;} --&gt;&lt;span class=&quot;text1865931Font1&quot;  &gt;If 2 joints in 1 finger were treated, perform the finger extension procedure on the affected MP joint before the affected PIP joint. &#x0A;&lt;/span &gt;&lt;/li &gt;&#x0A;&lt;li  class=&quot;text1865931Font1&quot; data-bltIndnt=&quot;0&quot; style=&quot;line-height:1.127;position:relative;margin-left:15px;margin-top:0px;margin-bottom:8px; list-style-image:url(&apos;images/text1865931SquareBulletFont7.png&apos;); padding-left:7px;&quot;&gt;&lt;!--{ &#x0A;data-bltIndnt:0&#x0A;} --&gt;&lt;span class=&quot;text1865931Font1&quot;  &gt;Local anesthesia may be used. &#x0A;&lt;/span &gt;&lt;/li &gt;&#x0A;&lt;li  class=&quot;text1865931Font1&quot; data-bltIndnt=&quot;0&quot; style=&quot;line-height:1.127;position:relative;margin-left:15px;margin-top:0px;margin-bottom:8px; list-style-image:url(&apos;images/text1865931SquareBulletFont7.png&apos;); padding-left:7px;&quot;&gt;&lt;!--{ &#x0A;data-bltIndnt:0&#x0A;} --&gt;&lt;span class=&quot;text1865931Font1&quot;  &gt;Care should be taken during release of contracture, as some patients may experience skin splitting; if this occurs, standard wound care is recommended. &#x0A;&lt;/span &gt;&lt;/li &gt;&#x0A;&lt;li  class=&quot;text1865931Font1&quot; data-bltIndnt=&quot;0&quot; style=&quot;line-height:1.127;position:relative;margin-left:15px;margin-top:0px;margin-bottom:8px; list-style-image:url(&apos;images/text1865931SquareBulletFont7.png&apos;); padding-left:7px;&quot;&gt;&lt;!--{ &#x0A;data-bltIndnt:0&#x0A;} --&gt;&lt;span class=&quot;text1865931Font1&quot;  &gt;While the patient&apos;s wrist is in the flexed position, apply moderate stretching pressure to the injected cord by extending the finger for approximately 10 to 20 seconds. For cords affecting the PIP joint, perform the finger extension procedure when the MP joint is in the flexed position. &#x0A;&lt;/span &gt;&lt;/li &gt;&#x0A;&lt;li  class=&quot;text1865931Font1&quot; data-bltIndnt=&quot;0&quot; style=&quot;line-height:1.127;position:relative;margin-left:15px;margin-top:0px;margin-bottom:8px; list-style-image:url(&apos;images/text1865931SquareBulletFont7.png&apos;); padding-left:7px;&quot;&gt;&lt;!--{ &#x0A;data-bltIndnt:0&#x0A;} --&gt;&lt;span class=&quot;text1865931Font1&quot;  &gt;HCPs should instruct patients to wear a splint at bedtime for up to 4 months. &#x0A;&lt;/span &gt;&lt;/li &gt;&#x0A;&lt;li  class=&quot;text1865931Font1&quot; data-bltIndnt=&quot;0&quot; style=&quot;line-height:1.127;position:relative;margin-left:15px;margin-top:0px;margin-bottom:8px; list-style-image:url(&apos;images/text1865931SquareBulletFont7.png&apos;); padding-left:7px;&quot;&gt;&lt;!--{ &#x0A;data-bltIndnt:0&#x0A;} --&gt;&lt;span class=&quot;text1865931Font1&quot;  &gt;HCPs should instruct patients to perform at-home finger exercises several times a day for several months. &#x0A;&lt;/span &gt;&lt;/li &gt;&#x0A;&lt;li  class=&quot;text1865931Font1&quot; data-bltIndnt=&quot;0&quot; style=&quot;line-height:1.127;position:relative;margin-left:15px;margin-top:0px;margin-bottom:8px; list-style-image:url(&apos;images/text1865931SquareBulletFont7.png&apos;); padding-left:7px;&quot;&gt;&lt;!--{ &#x0A;data-bltIndnt:0&#x0A;} --&gt;&lt;span class=&quot;text1865931Font1&quot;  &gt;If the first finger extension procedure does not result in disruption of the cord, a second and third attempt can be performed at 5- to 10-minute intervals. However, no more than 3 attempts per joint are recommended to disrupt a cord. &amp;nbsp;&#x0A;&lt;/span &gt;&lt;/li &gt;&#x0A;&lt;li  class=&quot;text1865931Font1&quot; data-bltIndnt=&quot;0&quot; style=&quot;line-height:1.127;position:relative;margin-left:15px;margin-top:0px;margin-bottom:0px; list-style-image:url(&apos;images/text1865931SquareBulletFont7.png&apos;); padding-left:7px;&quot;&gt;&lt;!--{ &#x0A;data-bltIndnt:0&#x0A;} --&gt;&lt;span class=&quot;text1865931Font1&quot;  &gt;Four weeks after the XIAFLEX injection and finger extension procedure, if an MP or PIP contracture remains, the cord may be re-injected with a single dose of 0.58 mg of XIAFLEX and the finger extension procedure may be repeated (approximately 24 to 72 hours after injection). Injections and finger extension procedures may be administered up to 3 times per cord at approximately 4-week intervals. &lt;br /&gt;&#x0A;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span &gt;&lt;/li &gt;&#x0A;&lt;/ul &gt;&#x0A;&lt;p  style=&quot;margin-left:0px;text-indent:0px;margin-top:0px;margin-bottom:8px;text-align:left;&quot; &gt;&#x0A;&lt;span class=&quot;text1865931Font2&quot;  &gt;Please view the video clip on the finger extension procedure &lt;/span &gt;&lt;a href=&quot;##Action 1889023&quot;  &gt;&lt;span class=&quot;text1865931Font3&quot;  &gt;here&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/a &gt;&lt;span class=&quot;text1865931Font4&quot;  &gt;.&lt;/span &gt;&lt;span class=&quot;text1865931Font1&quot; style=&quot;background-color:transparent&quot; &gt;&#x0A;&lt;/span &gt;&lt;/p &gt;&#x0A;</html>
<rtf>{\rtf1\ansi\deflang1033\ftnbj\uc1\deff1&#x0A;{\fonttbl{\f0 \froman \fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1 \fswiss Arial;}{\f2 \fnil \fcharset2 Wingdings;}{\f3 \froman \fcharset2 Symbol;}}&#x0A;{\colortbl\red0\green0\blue0 ;\red255\green255\blue255 ;\red0\green0\blue255 ;\red0\green0\blue0 ;}&#x0A;{\stylesheet{\f1\fs24\cf3\cb1\ulc3 Normal;}{\cs1\cf3\cb1\ulc3 Default Paragraph Font;}{\s2\snext0\cf3\cb1\ulc3 ;}}&#x0A;{\*\revtbl{Unknown;}}&#x0A;\paperw12240\paperh15840\margl1800\margr1800\margt1440\margb1440\headery720\footery720\deftab360\formshade\aendnotes\aftnnrlc\pgbrdrhead\pgbrdrfoot&#x0A;\sectd\pgwsxn12240\pghsxn15840\marglsxn1800\margrsxn1800\margtsxn1440\margbsxn1440\headery720\footery720\sbkpage\pgncont\pgndec&#x0A;\plain\plain\f1\fs24\sa120\ql At the follow-up visit anywhere from 24 to 72 hours after the injection(s), if a contracture still remains, passive finger extension may be performed on each treated joint to facilitate cord disruption. Key points to note &#x0A;include the following: \lang1033\hich\f1\loch\f1\par\pard\s0{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnhang\pnf2{\pntxtb \&apos;a7}}\fi-360\li360\sa120\ql\plain\f1\fs24\lang1033\hich\f1\loch\f1 If 2 joints in 1 finger were treated, perform the finger extension procedure on the affected &#x0A;MP joint before the affected PIP joint. \plain\f1\fs24\par\lang1033\hich\f1\loch\f1 Local anesthesia may be used. \plain\f1\fs24\par\lang1033\hich\f1\loch\f1 Care should be taken during release of contracture, as some patients may experience skin splitting; &#x0A;if this occurs, standard wound care is recommended. \plain\f1\fs24\par\lang1033\hich\f1\loch\f1 While the patient&apos;s wrist is in the flexed position, apply moderate stretching pressure to the injected cord by extending the finger for approximately 10 to &#x0A;20 seconds. For cords affecting the PIP joint, perform the finger extension procedure when the MP joint is in the flexed position. \plain\f1\fs24\par\lang1033\hich\f1\loch\f1 HCPs should instruct patients to wear a splint at bedtime for up to 4 months. \plain\f1\fs24\par\lang1033\hich\f1\loch\f1 &#x0A;HCPs should instruct patients to perform at-home finger exercises several times a day for several months. \plain\f1\fs24\par\lang1033\hich\f1\loch\f1 If the first finger extension procedure does not result in disruption of the cord, a second and third &#x0A;attempt can be performed at 5- to 10-minute intervals. However, no more than 3 attempts per joint are recommended to disrupt a cord.  \plain\f1\fs24\par\sa0\lang1033\hich\f1\loch\f1 Four weeks after the XIAFLEX injection and finger extension procedure, &#x0A;if an MP or PIP contracture remains, the cord may be re-injected with a single dose of 0.58 mg of XIAFLEX and the finger extension procedure may be repeated (approximately 24 to 72 hours after injection). Injections and finger extension procedures may &#x0A;be administered up to 3 times per cord at approximately 4-week intervals. \plain\f1\fs24\line\par\pard\s0\sa120\ql\b Please view the video clip on the finger extension procedure \cf2\sshlink\ul{\field{\fldinst HYPERLINK &quot;##Action 1889023&quot;}{\fldrslt here}}\cf3\sshlink0\ul0 &#x0A;.\plain\f1\fs24\par}&#x0A;</rtf>
<raw>﻿At the follow-up visit anywhere from 24 to 72 hours after the injection(s), if a contracture still remains, passive finger extension may be performed on each treated joint to facilitate cord disruption. Key points to note include the following: &#x0A;If 2 joints in 1 finger were treated, perform the finger extension procedure on the affected MP joint before the affected PIP joint. &#x0A;Local anesthesia may be used. &#x0A;Care should be taken during release of contracture, as some patients may experience skin splitting; if this occurs, standard wound care is recommended. &#x0A;While the patient&apos;s wrist is in the flexed position, apply moderate stretching pressure to the injected cord by extending the finger for approximately 10 to 20 seconds. For cords affecting the PIP joint, perform the finger extension procedure when the MP joint is in the flexed position. &#x0A;HCPs should instruct patients to wear a splint at bedtime for up to 4 months. &#x0A;HCPs should instruct patients to perform at-home finger exercises several times a day for several months. &#x0A;If the first finger extension procedure does not result in disruption of the cord, a second and third attempt can be performed at 5- to 10-minute intervals. However, no more than 3 attempts per joint are recommended to disrupt a cord.  &#x0A;Four weeks after the XIAFLEX injection and finger extension procedure, if an MP or PIP contracture remains, the cord may be re-injected with a single dose of 0.58 mg of XIAFLEX and the finger extension procedure may be repeated (approximately 24 to 72 hours after injection). Injections and finger extension procedures may be administered up to 3 times per cord at approximately 4-week intervals. &#x0F;&#x0A;Please view the video clip on the finger extension procedure here.</raw>
