<html>span.text1808312Font1 { font-size:16px; font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;, sans-serif; color:#010101;}&#x0A;span.text1808312Font2 { font-size:16px; font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;, sans-serif; color:#010101; font-style:italic;}&#x0A;span.text1808312Font3 { font-size:16px; font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;, sans-serif; color:#010101; font-weight:bold;}&#x0A;span.text1808312Font4 { font-size:16px; font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;, sans-serif; color:#0000ff; font-weight:bold; text-decoration:underline;}&#x0A;span.text1808312Font5 { font-size:16px; font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;, sans-serif; color:#000000; font-weight:bold;}&#x0A;&lt;p  legLH=&quot;1.127&quot; style=&quot;margin-left:0px;text-indent:0px;line-height:1.080;margin-top:0px;margin-bottom:0px;text-align:left;&quot; &gt;&#x0A;&lt;span class=&quot;text1808312Font1&quot;  &gt;The mechanism of action of XIAFLEX involves 2 microbial collagenases (AUX-I and AUX-II) that work together to hydrolyze collagen, resulting in lysis of collagen deposits. &lt;/span &gt;&lt;span class=&quot;text1808312Font2&quot;  &gt;In vitro&lt;/span &gt;&lt;span class=&quot;text1808312Font1&quot;  &gt; studies suggest XIAFLEX disrupts types I and III collagen. In both &lt;/span &gt;&lt;span class=&quot;text1808312Font2&quot;  &gt;in vitro&lt;/span &gt;&lt;span class=&quot;text1808312Font1&quot;  &gt; and &lt;/span &gt;&lt;span class=&quot;text1808312Font2&quot;  &gt;in vivo&lt;/span &gt;&lt;span class=&quot;text1808312Font1&quot;  &gt; studies and at higher doses and longer incubation times, Type IV collagen (non-fibrillar) was impacted through collagen lysis in small veins, without structural damage to arteries, nerves or large veins, which contain type IV collagen. However, there are no clinical data regarding the relative contributions of the individual collagenases (AUX-I or AUX-II) to the efficacy of XIAFLEX in the treatment of Dupuytren&apos;s contracture. &#x0A;&lt;/span &gt;&lt;/p &gt;&#x0A;&lt;p  legLH=&quot;1.127&quot; style=&quot;margin-left:0px;text-indent:0px;line-height:1.080;margin-top:0px;margin-bottom:0px;text-align:left;&quot; &gt;&#x0A;&lt;span class=&quot;text1808312Font1&quot; style=&quot;background-color:transparent&quot; &gt;&#x0A;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span &gt;&lt;/p &gt;&#x0A;&lt;p  style=&quot;margin-left:0px;text-indent:0px;margin-top:0px;margin-bottom:0px;text-align:left;&quot; &gt;&#x0A;&lt;span class=&quot;text1808312Font3&quot;  &gt;Please view the video of the mechanism of action for XIAFLEX &lt;/span &gt;&lt;a href=&quot;##Action 1890971&quot;  &gt;&lt;span class=&quot;text1808312Font4&quot;  &gt;here&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/a &gt;&lt;span class=&quot;text1808312Font5&quot;  &gt;. &amp;nbsp;&lt;/span &gt;&lt;span class=&quot;text1808312Font1&quot; style=&quot;background-color:transparent&quot; &gt;&#x0A;&lt;/span &gt;&lt;/p &gt;&#x0A;</html>
<rtf>{\rtf1\ansi\deflang1033\ftnbj\uc1\deff1&#x0A;{\fonttbl{\f0 \froman \fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1 \fswiss Arial;}}&#x0A;{\colortbl\red0\green0\blue0 ;\red255\green255\blue255 ;\red0\green0\blue255 ;\red0\green0\blue0 ;}&#x0A;{\stylesheet{\f1\fs24\cf3\cb1\ulc3 Normal;}{\cs1\cf3\cb1\ulc3 Default Paragraph Font;}{\s2\snext0\cf3\cb1\ulc3 ;}}&#x0A;{\*\revtbl{Unknown;}}&#x0A;\paperw12240\paperh15840\margl1800\margr1800\margt1440\margb1440\headery720\footery720\deftab360\formshade\aendnotes\aftnnrlc\pgbrdrhead\pgbrdrfoot&#x0A;\sectd\pgwsxn12240\pghsxn15840\marglsxn1800\margrsxn1800\margtsxn1440\margbsxn1440\headery720\footery720\sbkpage\pgncont\pgndec&#x0A;\plain\plain\f1\fs24\ql The mechanism of action of XIAFLEX involves 2 microbial collagenases (AUX-I and AUX-II) that work together to hydrolyze collagen, resulting in lysis of collagen deposits. \i In vitro\plain\f1\fs24  studies suggest XIAFLEX disrupts &#x0A;types I and III collagen. In both \i in vitro\plain\f1\fs24  and \i in vivo\plain\f1\fs24  studies and at higher doses and longer incubation times, Type IV collagen (non-fibrillar) was impacted through collagen lysis in small veins, without structural &#x0A;damage to arteries, nerves or large veins, which contain type IV collagen. However, there are no clinical data regarding the relative contributions of the individual collagenases (AUX-I or AUX-II) to the efficacy of XIAFLEX in the treatment of Dupuytren&apos;s &#x0A;contracture. \par\par\b Please view the video of the mechanism of action for XIAFLEX \cf2\sshlink\ul{\field{\fldinst HYPERLINK &quot;##Action 1890971&quot;}{\fldrslt here}}\cf3\sshlink0\ul0 .  \plain\f1\fs24\par}&#x0A;</rtf>
<raw>﻿The mechanism of action of XIAFLEX involves 2 microbial collagenases (AUX-I and AUX-II) that work together to hydrolyze collagen, resulting in lysis of collagen deposits. In vitro studies suggest XIAFLEX disrupts types I and III collagen. In both in vitro and in vivo studies and at higher doses and longer incubation times, Type IV collagen (non-fibrillar) was impacted through collagen lysis in small veins, without structural damage to arteries, nerves or large veins, which contain type IV collagen. However, there are no clinical data regarding the relative contributions of the individual collagenases (AUX-I or AUX-II) to the efficacy of XIAFLEX in the treatment of Dupuytren&apos;s contracture. &#x0A;Please view the video of the mechanism of action for XIAFLEX here.  </raw>
<numoflines>10</numoflines>
