<opacity>100</opacity>
<html>span.text1808998Font1 { font-size:16px; font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;, sans-serif; color:#010101;}&#x0A;&lt;p  legLH=&quot;1.127&quot; style=&quot;margin-left:0px;text-indent:0px;line-height:1.080;margin-top:0px;margin-bottom:0px;text-align:left;&quot; &gt;&#x0A;&lt;span class=&quot;text1808998Font1&quot;  &gt;Although recurrence of a palpable cord can occur following any treatment option, XIAFLEX has data on treating recurrent contractures. &amp;nbsp;&#x0A;&lt;/span &gt;&lt;/p &gt;&#x0A;&lt;p  legLH=&quot;1.127&quot; style=&quot;margin-left:0px;text-indent:0px;line-height:1.080;margin-top:0px;margin-bottom:0px;text-align:left;&quot; &gt;&#x0A;&lt;span class=&quot;text1808998Font1&quot; style=&quot;background-color:transparent&quot; &gt;&#x0A;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span &gt;&lt;/p &gt;&#x0A;&lt;p  legLH=&quot;1.127&quot; style=&quot;margin-left:0px;text-indent:0px;line-height:1.080;margin-top:0px;margin-bottom:0px;text-align:left;&quot; &gt;&#x0A;&lt;span class=&quot;text1808998Font1&quot;  &gt;Study 5 evaluated a subset of patients (n=52) from Study 4 of patients with a previous successfully treated joint which had recurrence. Patients received up to 3 XIAFLEX 0.58-mg injections. Recurrence in Study 4 was defined as an increase in joint contracture by at least 20Â° in the presence of a palpable cord, or those patients who underwent medical or surgical intervention to correct a new or worsening contracture in that joint. &#x0A;&lt;/span &gt;&lt;/p &gt;&#x0A;&lt;p  legLH=&quot;1.127&quot; style=&quot;margin-left:0px;text-indent:0px;line-height:1.080;margin-top:0px;margin-bottom:0px;text-align:left;&quot; &gt;&#x0A;&lt;span class=&quot;text1808998Font1&quot; style=&quot;background-color:transparent&quot; &gt;&#x0A;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span &gt;&lt;/p &gt;&#x0A;&lt;p  legLH=&quot;1.127&quot; style=&quot;margin-left:0px;text-indent:0px;line-height:1.080;margin-top:0px;margin-bottom:0px;text-align:left;&quot; &gt;&#x0A;&lt;span class=&quot;text1808998Font1&quot;  &gt;In this study, clinical success after retreatment was achieved in 65% of recurrent MP joints and 45% of recurrent PIP joints. While Study 5 retreated a small subset of patients from Study 4, it was inclusive of patients with recurrence with or without medical or surgical intervention. No new safety signals were identified among patients who were retreated with XIAFLEX. &#x0A;&lt;/span &gt;&lt;/p &gt;&#x0A;</html>
<rtf>{\rtf1\ansi\deflang1033\ftnbj\uc1\deff1&#x0A;{\fonttbl{\f0 \froman \fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1 \fswiss Arial;}}&#x0A;{\colortbl\red0\green0\blue0 ;\red255\green255\blue255 ;\red0\green0\blue0 ;}&#x0A;{\stylesheet{\f1\fs24\cf2\cb1\ulc2 Normal;}{\cs1\cf2\cb1\ulc2 Default Paragraph Font;}{\s2\snext0\cf2\cb1\ulc2 ;}}&#x0A;{\*\revtbl{Unknown;}}&#x0A;\paperw12240\paperh15840\margl1800\margr1800\margt1440\margb1440\headery720\footery720\deftab360\formshade\aendnotes\aftnnrlc\pgbrdrhead\pgbrdrfoot&#x0A;\sectd\pgwsxn12240\pghsxn15840\marglsxn1800\margrsxn1800\margtsxn1440\margbsxn1440\headery720\footery720\sbkpage\pgncont\pgndec&#x0A;\plain\plain\f1\fs24\ql\lang1033\hich\f1\loch\f1 Although recurrence of a palpable cord can occur following any treatment option, XIAFLEX has data on treating recurrent contractures.  \plain\f1\fs24\par\par\lang1033\hich\f1\loch\f1 Study 5 evaluated a &#x0A;subset of patients (n=52) from Study 4 of patients with a previous successfully treated joint which had recurrence. Patients received up to 3 XIAFLEX 0.58-mg injections. Recurrence in Study 4 was defined as an increase in joint contracture by at least &#x0A;20\&apos;b0 in the presence of a palpable cord, or those patients who underwent medical or surgical intervention to correct a new or worsening contracture in that joint. \plain\f1\fs24\par\par\lang1033\hich\f1\loch\f1 In this study, clinical success after &#x0A;retreatment was achieved in 65% of recurrent MP joints and 45% of recurrent PIP joints. While Study 5 retreated a small subset of patients from Study 4, it was inclusive of patients with recurrence with or without medical or surgical intervention. No &#x0A;new safety signals were identified among patients who were retreated with XIAFLEX. \plain\f1\fs24\par}&#x0A;</rtf>
<raw>﻿Although recurrence of a palpable cord can occur following any treatment option, XIAFLEX has data on treating recurrent contractures.  &#x0A;Study 5 evaluated a subset of patients (n=52) from Study 4 of patients with a previous successfully treated joint which had recurrence. Patients received up to 3 XIAFLEX 0.58-mg injections. Recurrence in Study 4 was defined as an increase in joint contracture by at least 20° in the presence of a palpable cord, or those patients who underwent medical or surgical intervention to correct a new or worsening contracture in that joint. &#x0A;In this study, clinical success after retreatment was achieved in 65% of recurrent MP joints and 45% of recurrent PIP joints. While Study 5 retreated a small subset of patients from Study 4, it was inclusive of patients with recurrence with or without medical or surgical intervention. No new safety signals were identified among patients who were retreated with XIAFLEX. </raw>
<numoflines>15</numoflines>
